  "question": {
    "text": "The number of non-polar molecules from the following is<br/><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HF</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>HCl</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>CHCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "4",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The molecules which have perfect geometry and no lone pairs are zero dipole moment molecules. Those molecules termed as non-polar molecules. Amongst the given compounds, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> are non polar molecules.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> has two lone pairs and two bond pairs. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> has two sigma bond pairs and one lone pair. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CHCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> has net dipole moment because the bond moments do not cancel with each other. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> have one lone pair and three bond pairs. In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HF</mi></math> bond moment is the dipole moment. Hence, all these molecules have some net dipole moment.</p><p>The non-polar molecules are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p>"
  }
}